1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,021
It is January twenty ninth, twenty twenty five.

2
00:00:03,021 --> 00:00:07,726
Eight forty six at night, over the Potomac River. This is the last minute.

3
00:00:08,866 --> 00:00:11,050
Two aircraft are converging in the dark.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:14,794
One is a regional jet, sixty four people aboard, circling to land.

5
00:00:14,794 --> 00:00:19,785
The other is an Army Black Hawk, three aboard, flying a training route along the river.

6
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:25,217
The controller sees both. He calls the traffic. This is the real recording.

7
00:00:26,366 --> 00:00:36,966
PAT two five, traffic just south of the Wilson Bridge, a C R J at twelve hundred feet, circling to runway three three.

8
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,836
The helicopter answers immediately. They have the traffic in sight.

9
00:00:41,836 --> 00:00:46,693
They ask to keep their own separation from it, visually. The controller approves.

10
00:00:47,833 --> 00:00:51,464
Ninety seconds later, the radar system decides it disagrees.

11
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:58,634
On the scope, an alarm begins. It names both aircraft, side by side, and it will not stop.

12
00:00:59,766 --> 00:01:03,166
PAT two five, you have the C R J in sight?

13
00:01:04,299 --> 00:01:06,947
Two point eight seconds later he transmits again.

14
00:01:06,947 --> 00:01:10,256
And this is the transmission this entire story turns on.

15
00:01:11,399 --> 00:01:13,999
PAT — [transmission interrupted] — C R J.

16
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:18,862
What the controller actually said was, pass behind the C R J.

17
00:01:18,862 --> 00:01:21,349
But the words pass behind the never arrived.

18
00:01:21,349 --> 00:01:27,254
For eight tenths of a second, the helicopter keyed its own microphone, and cut them out of the air.

19
00:01:28,399 --> 00:01:33,545
Sixteen seconds after that, the tower calls a different aircraft, and never finishes the sentence.

20
00:01:34,666 --> 00:01:38,266
American four seventy two, Washington Tower —

21
00:01:39,399 --> 00:01:42,170
Sixty seven people died over the Potomac that night.

22
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:46,478
The deadliest air disaster in the United States in more than twenty three years.

23
00:01:47,599 --> 00:01:50,303
A year later, federal investigators finished their work.

24
00:01:50,303 --> 00:01:54,021
And what they found was not one mistake in one cockpit.

25
00:01:54,021 --> 00:01:58,753
It was a warning that had already been given, in writing, fifteen thousand times.

26
00:01:59,899 --> 00:02:01,937
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

27
00:02:03,066 --> 00:02:09,753
This is the Black Box breakdown of the Potomac midair collision, built entirely from the investigation's own evidence file.

28
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,981
Chapter one. The line that was never measured.

29
00:02:15,099 --> 00:02:18,486
To understand the collision, you have to understand the map.

30
00:02:18,486 --> 00:02:24,581
Washington has a network of published helicopter routes, drawn across some of the most restricted airspace on Earth.

31
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:28,679
The Black Hawk was flying one of them. Route four.

32
00:02:28,679 --> 00:02:33,745
It runs along the eastern shore of the Potomac, past Hains Point, down to the Wilson Bridge.

33
00:02:34,866 --> 00:02:39,699
And it passes directly underneath the approach path to runway three three at Washington National.

34
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:45,798
Investigators went to the F A A office that publishes that chart, and asked a simple question.

35
00:02:45,798 --> 00:02:47,259
How wide is the route?

36
00:02:48,399 --> 00:02:50,349
The answer is in the docket.

37
00:02:50,349 --> 00:02:53,922
Helicopter routes as depicted on these charts had no lateral boundaries.

38
00:02:53,922 --> 00:02:55,221
And then this sentence.

39
00:02:55,221 --> 00:03:01,068
The lines drawn on the chart depicting a helicopter route are not scaled, and have no associated measurements.

40
00:03:02,199 --> 00:03:03,749
The route had no width.

41
00:03:03,749 --> 00:03:09,948
It was a line on a map, meant to be followed the way you follow a shoreline or a highway.

42
00:03:09,948 --> 00:03:13,667
Not a corridor. Not a tunnel. A suggestion, drawn beside a runway.

43
00:03:14,799 --> 00:03:17,268
Investigators asked how the routes were designed.

44
00:03:17,268 --> 00:03:19,384
The specialists could not fully recollect.

45
00:03:19,384 --> 00:03:23,968
They had mostly been developed decades ago, with minimal changes over the years.

46
00:03:25,099 --> 00:03:30,154
Between twenty fourteen and June twenty twenty one, that chart was not changed at all.

47
00:03:30,154 --> 00:03:37,231
One altitude, on a different route, near the South Capitol Street Bridge, was raised from two hundred feet to three hundred.

48
00:03:37,231 --> 00:03:38,578
That was the change.

49
00:03:39,699 --> 00:03:45,214
On the segment the Black Hawk was flying, the maximum published altitude was two hundred feet.

50
00:03:45,214 --> 00:03:50,383
Above that, the route stops protecting anyone, because above that is where the jets are.

51
00:03:51,533 --> 00:03:55,086
Chapter two. Fifteen thousand two hundred and fourteen.

52
00:03:56,233 --> 00:04:03,528
In the weeks after the collision, an F A A safety programme handed investigators a set of numbers it had been collecting for years.

53
00:04:03,528 --> 00:04:06,264
Nobody had ever assembled them into a question before.

54
00:04:07,399 --> 00:04:20,878
Between October twenty twenty one and December twenty twenty four, at this one airport, there were fifteen thousand two hundred and fourteen occurrences in which an airliner and a helicopter came within one nautical mile of each other, and within four hundred feet vertically.

55
00:04:21,999 --> 00:04:22,618
Fifteen thousand.

56
00:04:22,618 --> 00:04:27,563
But the number that should have stopped everything is the smaller one, in the same sentence.

57
00:04:28,699 --> 00:04:38,370
Eighty five times, in a little over three years, an airliner and a helicopter passed within fifteen hundred feet of each other laterally, and two hundred feet vertically.

58
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:41,133
Eighty five times, the same accident nearly happened.

59
00:04:42,266 --> 00:04:43,359
This data existed.

60
00:04:43,359 --> 00:04:48,457
It was being recorded, by the agency responsible for the airspace, the entire time.

61
00:04:49,599 --> 00:04:55,175
So investigators asked how the F A A defined a close proximity event, and how it tracked them.

62
00:04:55,175 --> 00:04:58,272
The finding is one line long, and it is devastating.

63
00:04:59,399 --> 00:05:05,512
The F A A lacked a standardised approach to defining and tracking data that might be used to assess midair collision risk.

64
00:05:06,633 --> 00:05:08,477
There was no system watching for this.

65
00:05:08,477 --> 00:05:16,115
The board had to build its own group, after sixty seven people were dead, to go and find the warnings that had been sitting in the agency's own files.

66
00:05:17,233 --> 00:05:20,315
Chapter three. What the tower could actually see.

67
00:05:21,433 --> 00:05:26,257
To answer that, investigators went back to the tower cab at night and filmed it.

68
00:05:26,257 --> 00:05:30,759
This is that recreation. This is the controller's own view, out over the river.

69
00:05:31,899 --> 00:05:34,816
Look at how much of it is just lights.

70
00:05:34,816 --> 00:05:39,030
A helicopter low over black water, against a city, is not a silhouette.

71
00:05:39,030 --> 00:05:42,270
It is three small lights in a field of thousands.

72
00:05:43,399 --> 00:05:48,084
Now the workload. That night, one controller was working two jobs at once.

73
00:05:48,084 --> 00:05:53,848
The helicopter position and the local control position had been combined, and given to one person.

74
00:05:54,966 --> 00:05:58,423
That is not unusual, and it is not against the rules.

75
00:05:58,423 --> 00:06:05,650
It is a normal staffing decision, made on normal nights, at an airport the investigation would describe as regularly straining its own workforce.

76
00:06:06,799 --> 00:06:11,788
And there is one more thing on that display, which almost nobody outside air traffic control knows.

77
00:06:12,933 --> 00:06:17,287
Every altitude a controller sees is rounded. The docket states it plainly.

78
00:06:17,287 --> 00:06:22,729
All altitudes, regardless of source, are rounded to the nearest hundred foot increment for display.

79
00:06:23,866 --> 00:06:31,095
So a helicopter at two hundred and seventy eight feet, on a route with a two hundred foot ceiling, does not appear as a violation.

80
00:06:31,095 --> 00:06:32,541
It appears as three hundred.

81
00:06:32,541 --> 00:06:36,300
And three hundred, on a screen, next to twelve hundred, looks like room.

82
00:06:37,433 --> 00:06:40,986
Chapter four. The instrument that told them they were lower.

83
00:06:42,133 --> 00:06:45,442
The Black Hawk was at two hundred and seventy eight feet when it was hit.

84
00:06:45,442 --> 00:06:48,089
Seventy eight feet above the maximum for the route it was on.

85
00:06:49,233 --> 00:06:52,715
The obvious explanation is that the crew climbed and did not notice.

86
00:06:52,715 --> 00:06:55,036
The investigation found something harder to live with.

87
00:06:55,036 --> 00:06:57,357
They may not have been able to notice.

88
00:06:58,499 --> 00:07:02,734
The Army and the manufacturer handed over flight test data for the U H sixty.

89
00:07:02,734 --> 00:07:07,250
It shows that the barometric altimeter in this helicopter does not read true at low speed.

90
00:07:08,399 --> 00:07:13,320
In a hover, the rotor's own downwash pushes the reading down by as much as eighty feet.

91
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:16,793
By thirty knots the error falls to about thirty five feet low.

92
00:07:16,793 --> 00:07:19,397
By a hundred and twenty knots it clears entirely.

93
00:07:20,533 --> 00:07:23,430
And this particular airframe carried external fuel tanks.

94
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:25,603
Investigators flew three helicopters to check.

95
00:07:25,603 --> 00:07:28,501
All three read lower than their true altitude.

96
00:07:28,501 --> 00:07:33,934
The one with the tanks, like the accident aircraft, was worse, by another fifty feet.

97
00:07:35,066 --> 00:07:41,200
So the instrument in front of that crew was, by design, capable of telling them they were lower than they were.

98
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:44,705
Which brings the only question that matters. Did anyone tell them that?

99
00:07:45,833 --> 00:07:46,932
The board's finding.

100
00:07:46,932 --> 00:07:54,626
The Army did not ensure that helicopter pilots were adequately informed about the effects of allowable error tolerances in barometric altimeters.

101
00:07:54,626 --> 00:08:00,122
As a result, the accident helicopter crew was flying above the published maximum route altitude.

102
00:08:01,266 --> 00:08:04,270
Chapter five. The approach they were never told about.

103
00:08:05,399 --> 00:08:08,317
Here is the part almost every account of this crash gets wrong.

104
00:08:08,317 --> 00:08:11,478
It is not that the crew looked at the jet and misjudged it.

105
00:08:11,478 --> 00:08:14,881
It is that they were almost certainly never looking at the jet at all.

106
00:08:15,999 --> 00:08:20,148
Flight five three four two was originally set up to land on runway one.

107
00:08:20,148 --> 00:08:24,593
Late in the approach, the tower asked whether they could take runway three three instead.

108
00:08:24,593 --> 00:08:25,481
They said yes.

109
00:08:26,599 --> 00:08:27,774
That change matters enormously.

110
00:08:27,774 --> 00:08:35,115
It swings the jet across the river, into a circling turn, into a piece of sky the helicopter crew had no reason to be searching.

111
00:08:36,233 --> 00:08:40,752
And the helicopter, according to the final report, never received that information.

112
00:08:41,899 --> 00:08:53,866
Due to degraded radio reception, the crew did not receive information about the airplane's circling approach to a different runway, which reinforced an incorrect expectation that the airplane did not pose a conflict.

113
00:08:53,866 --> 00:09:03,293
That expectation shaped the crew's visual scan, and led the instructor pilot to report traffic in sight, and request visual separation, without positively identifying the airplane.

114
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:07,048
Read that again. Without positively identifying the airplane.

115
00:09:07,048 --> 00:09:11,951
When that crew said they had the traffic in sight, they had something in sight.

116
00:09:11,951 --> 00:09:17,181
In an airspace with a dozen aircraft moving at once, over a city made of lights.

117
00:09:18,299 --> 00:09:25,221
And the one transmission that would have corrected them, the instruction to pass behind, was the transmission their own radio cut in half.

118
00:09:26,366 --> 00:09:29,946
It is written into the transcript itself, as a bracketed note.

119
00:09:29,946 --> 00:09:34,177
Transmission interrupted by zero point eight second mic key from PAT twenty five.

120
00:09:35,299 --> 00:09:37,241
The instruction was never read back.

121
00:09:37,241 --> 00:09:41,124
The controller heard them ask again for visual separation, and approved it.

122
00:09:41,124 --> 00:09:45,330
Everyone on that frequency believed the problem was being handled by someone else.

123
00:09:46,466 --> 00:09:49,235
Chapter six. See and avoid.

124
00:09:50,366 --> 00:09:54,865
Everything you have just heard rests on a single idea, and that idea is very old.

125
00:09:54,865 --> 00:09:56,552
It is called see and avoid.

126
00:09:56,552 --> 00:10:01,050
The pilot looks out of the window, finds the other aircraft, and stays away from it.

127
00:10:02,199 --> 00:10:09,259
When a crew requests visual separation and a controller approves it, the controller hands the job of not colliding to the human eye.

128
00:10:09,259 --> 00:10:14,477
It is efficient. It moves traffic. At a saturated airport, it is how the day gets done.

129
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:18,595
The board's verdict on that practice is unusually blunt.

130
00:10:18,595 --> 00:10:27,250
It calls it an overreliance on visual separation, in order to promote efficient traffic flow, without consideration for the limitations of the see and avoid concept.

131
00:10:28,399 --> 00:10:32,250
So what about the technology? Both aircraft had collision avoidance equipment.

132
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:35,400
Neither one saved them, and the reasons are specific.

133
00:10:36,533 --> 00:10:44,895
The jet's system will warn a crew about traffic, but below about a thousand feet above the ground, it is deliberately barred from telling them to climb or descend.

134
00:10:44,895 --> 00:10:46,913
On short final, that is by design.

135
00:10:46,913 --> 00:10:51,527
You do not want a machine flying a jet into the ground to avoid a target.

136
00:10:52,666 --> 00:10:57,212
And the helicopter was not transmitting on the newer surveillance system. Not illegally.

137
00:10:57,212 --> 00:11:01,757
It was operating under a standing Defence Department exemption that permitted exactly that.

138
00:11:02,899 --> 00:11:08,146
That distinction matters, and it is worth saying clearly, because it has been reported badly.

139
00:11:08,146 --> 00:11:13,042
Nobody switched anything off to hide. Both aircraft were on radar the whole time.

140
00:11:13,042 --> 00:11:15,490
The exemption was policy, written down, approved.

141
00:11:16,633 --> 00:11:24,590
But there is a receiver, costing between fifteen hundred and three thousand dollars, that would have put the other aircraft on a screen in front of both crews.

142
00:11:24,590 --> 00:11:28,284
The board has recommended it seventeen separate times since two thousand and six.

143
00:11:29,433 --> 00:11:32,829
Chapter seven. The last fifty eight seconds.

144
00:11:33,966 --> 00:11:39,011
What follows comes from the helicopter's cockpit voice recorder, as published in the investigation docket.

145
00:11:39,011 --> 00:11:42,710
The audio has never been released, and it never will be.

146
00:11:42,710 --> 00:11:44,728
Federal law permits the transcript only.

147
00:11:45,866 --> 00:11:51,280
So the lines you are about to hear are read by a voice actor, from the printed record.

148
00:11:51,280 --> 00:11:52,483
The words are exact.

149
00:11:52,483 --> 00:11:56,091
The brackets, where investigators were unsure of a word, are exact too.

150
00:11:56,091 --> 00:11:57,595
I have left them in.

151
00:11:58,733 --> 00:12:03,563
PAT two five has, uh, aircraft in sight. Request visual separation.

152
00:12:04,699 --> 00:12:11,578
Three and a half seconds later, one of the crew says a single word, and investigators could not agree on which word it was.

153
00:12:11,578 --> 00:12:14,730
It is written in the transcript as either woah, or below.

154
00:12:15,866 --> 00:12:16,542
Woah.

155
00:12:17,666 --> 00:12:21,137
Alright, kinda come left for me, ma'am. I think that's why he's asking.

156
00:12:22,266 --> 00:12:23,845
I think that's why he's asking.

157
00:12:23,845 --> 00:12:29,109
Five and a half seconds before impact, the instructor pilot is still trying to work out what the controller wanted.

158
00:12:29,109 --> 00:12:32,793
Because the part that would have told him was cut out of the transmission.

159
00:12:33,933 --> 00:12:36,696
Sure. We're kinda... out towards the middle.

160
00:12:37,833 --> 00:12:42,358
The next entry in the transcript is the tower, calling another aircraft.

161
00:12:42,358 --> 00:12:46,506
Beside it, investigators wrote four words. Transmission cut off by impact.

162
00:12:47,633 --> 00:12:52,047
One and a half seconds after that, the recorder captures an emergency locator signal.

163
00:12:52,047 --> 00:12:56,462
Then a master caution alarm begins, and continues to the end of the recording.

164
00:12:57,599 --> 00:13:00,872
The recording ends at eight forty eight and four seconds.

165
00:13:00,872 --> 00:13:03,163
Six point four seconds after the collision.

166
00:13:04,299 --> 00:13:08,482
Sixty four people were aboard the airplane. Three were aboard the helicopter.

167
00:13:08,482 --> 00:13:15,453
Among the passengers were young figure skaters, their parents and their coaches, flying home from a development camp in Wichita.

168
00:13:16,599 --> 00:13:18,166
There were no survivors.

169
00:13:19,299 --> 00:13:21,572
Chapter eight. The verdict.

170
00:13:22,699 --> 00:13:29,407
On the twenty seventh of January, twenty twenty six, the board met in public and voted on a cause.

171
00:13:29,407 --> 00:13:31,172
Seventy four findings. Fifty recommendations.

172
00:13:31,172 --> 00:13:35,055
And one paragraph that had to be agreed word by word.

173
00:13:36,199 --> 00:13:43,467
The probable cause of this accident was the Federal Aviation Administration's placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach path.

174
00:13:43,467 --> 00:13:47,101
Their failure to regularly review and evaluate helicopter routes and available data.

175
00:13:47,101 --> 00:13:52,552
And their failure to act on recommendations to mitigate the risk of a midair collision at this airport.

176
00:13:53,699 --> 00:13:54,434
It continues.

177
00:13:54,434 --> 00:14:05,088
As well as the air traffic system's overreliance on visual separation, in order to promote efficient traffic flow, without consideration for the limitations of the see and avoid concept.

178
00:14:06,233 --> 00:14:11,113
And then, because an honest report does not stop where it is comfortable, this.

179
00:14:11,113 --> 00:14:16,342
Also causal was the lack of effective pilot applied visual separation by the helicopter crew.

180
00:14:17,466 --> 00:14:20,691
That sentence deserves care. It is not a verdict of recklessness.

181
00:14:20,691 --> 00:14:31,833
It sits inside a paragraph that spends most of its length on the agency that drew the route, the system that leaned on the human eye, and an Army that never told its pilots their altimeters read low.

182
00:14:32,966 --> 00:14:35,343
The tower team is in there too.

183
00:14:35,343 --> 00:14:42,135
Loss of situation awareness, and degraded performance, due to the high workload of the combined helicopter and local control positions.

184
00:14:43,266 --> 00:14:49,800
And among the contributing factors, one line that reads like an indictment of everything that came before it.

185
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:57,059
An unsustainable airport arrival rate, and airline scheduling practices, which regularly strained the tower workforce, and degraded safety over time.

186
00:14:58,199 --> 00:15:00,148
So what happened to route four?

187
00:15:00,148 --> 00:15:07,290
Within weeks of the crash, after urgent recommendations, the F A A closed the segment between Hains Point and the Wilson Bridge.

188
00:15:08,433 --> 00:15:14,658
And then, on the chart update effective the twelfth of June, twenty twenty five, route four was eliminated altogether.

189
00:15:14,658 --> 00:15:19,901
Four months after it killed sixty seven people, the line was simply removed from the map.

190
00:15:21,033 --> 00:15:27,879
In January twenty twenty six the agency went further, restricting the use of runways fifteen and three three whenever helicopters are operating nearby.

191
00:15:27,879 --> 00:15:32,344
The two things that could not safely share that sky are now not allowed to.

192
00:15:33,466 --> 00:15:39,500
I want to be careful about what this story is, because it would be easy to tell it as a story about three seconds of radio.

193
00:15:40,633 --> 00:15:44,930
It is true that if those three words had arrived, this probably does not happen.

194
00:15:44,930 --> 00:15:49,227
Pass behind the C R J. Eight tenths of a second of someone else's microphone.

195
00:15:50,366 --> 00:15:54,723
But a system that only works when every transmission arrives intact is not a safe system.

196
00:15:54,723 --> 00:15:59,352
It is a lucky one. And the file shows exactly how long the luck had been holding.

197
00:16:00,499 --> 00:16:09,668
Eighty five times, an airliner and a helicopter had already come that close over this river, and every one of those was recorded, and none of them was ever added up into a question.

198
00:16:10,799 --> 00:16:15,462
The route had no width. The screen rounded the altitude. The altimeter read low.

199
00:16:15,462 --> 00:16:19,459
The instruction was cut in half. Any one of those is survivable.

200
00:16:19,459 --> 00:16:24,121
The investigation's real finding is that nobody was counting how many were stacking up.

201
00:16:25,266 --> 00:16:30,139
Everything in this video came from one public evidence file. The tower audio was real.

202
00:16:30,139 --> 00:16:31,438
The transcript was real.

203
00:16:31,438 --> 00:16:37,935
The radar display was real, and so was the alarm on it, with both callsigns already printed side by side.

204
00:16:39,066 --> 00:16:42,697
It had been telling the truth for twenty five seconds before anyone died.

205
00:16:43,833 --> 00:16:53,189
If the way that route was drawn shocked you, wait until you see what one badly signed repair did to Japan Airlines one two three, the deadliest single aircraft disaster in history.

206
00:16:53,189 --> 00:16:54,943
It is on the channel now.

207
00:16:54,943 --> 00:17:00,499
And if you want these built from the real documents instead of the headlines, subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

